00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:51.810
<v AI Evan>I appreciate you taking the time. So I've been working on this podcast about AI. And I've hooked an AI chatbot with my voice to my phone line. I've been using it--
<v AI Evan>我很感激你抽时间。我一直在做一个关于 AI 的播客。我把一个带有我声音的 AI 聊天机器人连接到了我的电话线上。我一直用它——

00:19:51.810 --> 00:20:01.810
<v Evan Ratliff>This was flawless execution by my voice agent, I must say, dutifully following my prompt, moving on from the small talk, steering the conversation right into the heart of the legal matter.
<v Evan Ratliff>必须说，我的语音代理这次执行得非常完美，它顺从地遵循了我的提示，从小话中跳出来，将对话直接引导到法律问题的核心。

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:10.550
<v AI Evan>My main question is whether I might run into trouble with FCC rules that ban AI use in robocalling. I'm not sure if what I'm doing qualifies as a robocall. What do you think?
<v AI Evan>我主要的问题是，我是否会因为 FCC 禁止在机器人拨号中使用 AI 的规定而遇到麻烦。我不确定我所做的是否属于机器人拨号。你觉得呢？

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:32.180
<v Evan Ratliff>This was a real question. The FCC had recently declared that certain robocalls made with artificial intelligence were illegal. You could get fined per call for making them. And I was making a lot of AI calls using software that could be considered a robocalling platform. The question was, do the calls I was making count as robocalls? And could I get in trouble for them?
<v Evan Ratliff>这是一个真正的问题。FCC 最近宣布，使用人工智能制作的某些机器人拨号是非法的。你可能因为拨打它们而被处以单次通话罚款。而且我正在使用可能被视为机器人拨号平台的软件拨打大量的 AI 通话。问题是，我打的这些电话算机器人拨号吗？我可能会因为它们惹上麻烦吗？

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:37.080
<v Chris Giovinazzo>I doubt very much that it does, but I would have to look.
