<v AI Evan>As you probably know, I'm recording this for a podcast.
<v AI Evan>正如你可能知道的，我正在为播客录制这个。

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:20.080
<v Lawyer>Great.
<v Lawyer>太棒了。

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:22.790
<v Evan Ratliff>This guy had just graduated from law school.
<v Evan Ratliff>这家伙刚从法学院毕业。

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:27.820
<v AI Evan>How do you feel about AI's potential in the legal field?
<v AI Evan>你如何看待 AI 在法律领域的潜力？

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:51.280
<v Lawyer>I would hope that I didn't just go into hundreds of thousands of dollars of debt and spend all this time and energy-- and I'm currently studying for the bar-- to get into a job that could be done by a robot. And not only to do with being a lawyer, but anything, like stories, novelists, or playwrights.
<v Lawyer>我希望我没有只是背负几十万美元的债务，并花费了所有的时间和精力——我目前正在为律师资格考试做准备——去从事一份可以由机器人完成的工作。而且这不仅与作为律师有关，还与任何事情有关，比如写故事、小说家或剧作家。

00:24:51.280 --> 00:25:20.020
<v Lawyer>I would hope that we will be continually able to distinguish between work done by robots and work done by humans. I-- I don't know. There's something simultaneously very cynical and sad and also very just kind of eerie and scary about a world where the overlap is complete, and it's impossible to distinguish.
<v Lawyer>我希望我们将能够持续区分机器人完成的工作和人类完成的工作。我——我不知道。在一个重叠部分完全一致，且无法区分的世界里，有些东西既是非常愤世嫉俗和悲伤的，也是非常诡异和可怕的。

00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:26.090
<v Evan Ratliff>They talked for more than five minutes. And then something about the conversation seemed off to him.
<v Evan Ratliff>他们聊了超过五分钟。然后对话中的某些东西对他来说显得有些不对劲。

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:31.830
<v Lawyer>Is this-- are you AI? Is this a robot that I'm talking to right now?
<v Lawyer>这是——你是 AI 吗？我现在是在和一个机器人通话吗？

