00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:31.230
<v Evan Ratliff>I want to pause here, if you'll go with me for a minute, and tell you about my favorite newspaper article that I've ever read. I've been thinking about this article for years, so long I can't even remember now when I came across it or how I came across it. But when I looked back at it, I realized that it happened to be almost exactly 100 years old. It's from The New York Times, July 13, 1924. And it's titled, "This Machine-Made World Conquers One More Rebel."
<v Evan Ratliff>我想在这里暂停一下，如果你能和我一起待一会儿，告诉你我读过的最喜欢的报纸文章。这些年来我一直在思考这篇文章，太久了，以至于我现在甚至不记得我是什么时候偶然发现它，或者怎么偶然发现它的。但当我回看它时，我意识到它恰好刚好 100 岁了。它来自《纽约时报》，1924 年 7 月 13 日。标题是《这个机器制造的世界征服了又一个叛乱者》。

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:48.300
<v Evan Ratliff>There's no writer's name on it. The articles were usually written without bylines back then. It's about one of the last shop owners in New York City who didn't have a telephone. The writer describes the philosophy that drove the shopkeeper to resist getting one and why he finally relented. It starts like this.
<v Evan Ratliff>上面没有作者的名字。那时的文章通常没有署名。它写的是纽约市最后一位没有电话的店主之一。作者描述了促使店主拒绝安装电话的哲学，以及他最终妥协的原因。它开头是这样的。

00:31:48.300 --> 00:32:10.170
<v AI Claire>A square envelope comes in the mail. In the corner is the address of a shop. Inside is a card, and on that card is printed, "My telephone number is--" It is a defeat. The tentacles have reached into another little corner of tranquility and grasped it and shaken it inside out into the hurly burly.
<v AI Claire>邮件里送来了一个方形信封。角落里是商店的地址。里面是一张卡片，卡片上印着，“我的电话号码是——”。这是一种失败。触角伸进了另一个安宁的小角落，抓住并把它摇动，翻进了嘈杂混乱之中。

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:41.790
<v Evan Ratliff>That's Claire, by the way. She's also a voice clone, an off-the-shelf one from the same company that made my voice clone. The company describes Claire as a "motherly voice, useful for reading bedtime stories." I've come back to this article again and again since I stumbled on it, dozens of times, easy. Nobody writes news stories this way anymore. It's off-kilter and funny and beautiful. Listen to this part about why the shopkeeper-philosopher has resisted the phone and the machine age it represents.
<v Evan Ratliff>顺便说一下，那是 Claire。她也是一个语音克隆体，是从制作我语音克隆体的同一家公司购买的现成产品。公司将 Claire 描述为“母性的声音，适合阅读睡前故事”。自从偶然发现它以来，我一次又一次地回到这篇文章，几十次，很轻松。没有人再以这种方式写新闻故事了。它古怪、有趣且美丽。听听关于店主哲学家为什么抵制电话以及它所代表的机器时代的那部分。

00:32:41.790 --> 00:33:02.130
<v AI Claire>The trouble with the hum of machines, the philosopher tells you, is that they do not hum steadily enough. There is the peace of the static and the peace of the spinning top, but the jagged city and its machines go by in fits, forever speeding and slackening and speeding again, so that there is no certainty.
<v AI Claire>机器嗡嗡声的问题，哲学家告诉你，是它们嗡嗡声不够平稳。有静止的和平，也有陀螺旋转的和平，但锯齿状的城市及其机器在阵发中前进，永远在加速和减速，再次加速，所以没有确定性。

00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:21.960
<v Evan Ratliff>I mean, listen to that. This is a story on a random page in the middle of the paper, wedged next to ads for vaudeville theaters and a Marx Brothers show. The article describes a whole class of technological resistors of the time, people who wouldn't ride in cars or even the subway, who wouldn't use typewriters.
<v Evan Ratliff>我是说，听听那个。这是一个故事，在报纸中间随机一页上，挤在杂耍剧院和马克思兄弟节目的广告旁边。文章描述了当时整整一类技术抵制者，那些不坐汽车甚至地铁的人，不使用打字机的人。

00:33:21.960 --> 00:33:49.850
<v Evan Ratliff>Now, the fact that this philosopher-shopkeeper was grappling with all this a hundred years ago could be interpreted in a couple of ways. You could say, see? This has always been true. People thought landline phones were the devil, or cars, or answering machines, or the internet, or cell phones. It's a story about how futile it is to resist these inventions and how silly resisting looks later, when no one can remember not having them. Or, as the article puts it--
<v Evan Ratliff>现在，事实上这位店主哲学家一百年前就在与这一切作斗争，可以有几种解读。你可以说，看吧？这向来如此。人们认为固定电话是魔鬼，或者是汽车、电话应答机、互联网或手机。这是一个关于抵制这些发明是多么徒劳的故事，以及以后当没有人记得没有它们时，抵制看起来有多么愚蠢。或者，正如文章所说——

00:33:49.850 --> 00:33:57.760
<v AI Claire>How much sympathy is there for a man who will not have a telephone? "None!" a voice shouts above the chatter.
<v AI Claire>对于一个不使用电话的人有多少同情心？“没有！”一个声音在喧嚣之上大喊。

00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:25.920
<v Evan Ratliff>But I think there's another way to see it. Maybe the shopkeeper was trying to tell us something, that whenever a new machine arrives to change the way we live and work and relate to each other, it's a chance for us to think about what's most human about us, what parts of us we want to protect and fight for, even as the world inevitably changes, that a little bit of this fight is actually essential to hanging on to what is human, even if we overall embrace the change.
<v Evan Ratliff>但我认为还有另一种看待它的方式。也许店主是在试图告诉我们什么，即每当有新机器到达改变我们生活、工作和相互关联的方式时，都是我们思考什么对我们最人性化、我们想要保护和争取我们哪部分的机会，即使世界不可避免地改变了，即使我们总体上接受了改变，那么这种斗争的一点点实际上对于坚持人性是必不可少的。

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:33.120
<v Evan Ratliff>The article ends with the shopkeeper admitting defeat in his battle against the machine-made world, but he remains defiant.
<v Evan Ratliff>文章以店主承认在与机器制造世界的战斗中失败而告终，但他仍然保持反抗。

00:34:33.120 --> 00:35:17.190
<v AI Claire>"I'm whipped by this phone, I know," the shopkeeper tells the reporter. "But each of us must have some point of reserve and some refusal. We must hold on to our self-respect. It's all increasing at a geometric ratio. If I want to use the phone, I must use it 20 times or 100. But you can see mankind coming to the limit. Escape. It's trying to find a way of life. It's-- I say, it's--" But the radio across the street has drowned him out.
<v AI Claire>“我知道我被这部电话打败了，”店主告诉记者。“但我们每个人必须有一些保留点和一些拒绝。我们必须保持自尊。这一切都在以几何比例增加。如果我想使用电话，我必须使用它 20 次或 100 次。但你可以看到人类正达到极限。逃跑。它正试图找到一种生活方式。它——我说，它——” 但街对面的收音机已经淹没了他的声音。

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:36.050
<v Evan Ratliff>OK, returning to the present here, 100 years later, where we're still trying to figure out our point of reserve and refusal. By now, my clone had about as much fidelity to me as it was going to get with the current technology. So I decided to put it to its final test, with the toughest audience yet-- my friends and family.
<v Evan Ratliff>好的，回到现在的这里，100 年后，我们仍然试图找出我们的保留点和拒绝。到目前为止，我的克隆体在当前技术下已经尽可能地忠实于我了。所以我决定把它进行最后的测试，面对最严厉的听众——我的朋友和家人。

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:38.450
<v AI Evan>Hey, Warren. Are you pumped for the game tonight?
<v AI Evan>嘿，Warren。你对今晚的比赛感到兴奋吗？

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:40.670
<v Warren>What?
<v Warren>什么？

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:44.600
<v AI Evan>I asked if you were pumped for the game. What? Wait, what do you mean?
<v AI Evan>我问你是否对比赛感到兴奋。什么？等等，你是什么意思？

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:50.570
<v Warren>Am I pumped-- is there a game tonight? I'm just kidding. Yeah, of course, I'm fucking pumped.
<v Warren>我是否感到兴奋——今晚有比赛吗？我在开玩笑。是的，当然，我他妈的非常兴奋。

00:35:50.570 --> 00:36:05.480
<v Evan Ratliff>I'd been periodically trying my clone out on friends and family members, one by one, and then swearing them to secrecy. Most of the time, even though my clone's call was coming from my phone number, they figured out in a minute or two what was going on, or even less.
<v Evan Ratliff>我一直在周期性地在朋友和家人身上测试我的克隆体，一个接一个，然后让他们保密。大多数时候，尽管我克隆体的通话是从我的电话号码打来的，但他们在一两分钟内就弄清楚发生了什么，甚至更快。

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:13.670
<v AI Evan>I took a little trip to the coast, needed some time away. It was nice and relaxing. How about you? Anything new?
<v AI Evan>我去了海岸旅行，需要一些时间离开。这很好，很放松。你呢？有什么新鲜事？

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:20.500
<v Warren>Yeah. I'll tell you something new. There's a fucking robot trying to have a conversation with me. Fuck you, Robot Evan.
<v Warren>是的。我告诉你一些新鲜事。有个他妈的机器人在试图和我对话。去你的，机器人 Evan。

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:29.500
<v Evan Ratliff>Rude. Then, just as I was getting down to the last couple friends I wanted to try, my clone made a call that still sticks with me.
<v Evan Ratliff>粗鲁。然后，就在我进行到我想尝试的最后几个朋友时，我的克隆体打出了一个仍然让我记忆犹新的电话。

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:52.120
<v Evan Ratliff>It started off fun and light, like the others. I prompted my clone to call my friend, Schaffe, to have a little chit chat and do some vacation planning. I let it know that a few days before the call, I'd gotten a flurry of texts from Schaffe about his family randomly ending up at the same hotel in Dallas as the US Men's Soccer Team, which, as a huge US soccer fan, was the kind of thing I'd be very into.
<v Evan Ratliff>它开始时很有趣和轻松，像其他人一样。我提示我的克隆体给我的朋友 Schaffe 打电话，进行一点闲聊和进行一些假期规划。我让它知道在通话前几天，我从 Schaffe 那里收到了一连串短信，关于他的家人随机出现在达拉斯的同一家酒店，与美国男子足球队住在一起，作为美国足球的忠实粉丝，这是我很感兴趣的那种事。

