<v AI Evan>Perfect. We've got so much to catch up on. I was thinking, besides soaking up some sun at the beach, we should definitely hit up Agave for dinner and have a night out at Irish Eyes. Oh, and we can't miss the game on Thursday night. Sound good?
<v AI Evan>完美。我们有太多要聊的了。我想，除了在海滩晒晒太阳外，我们绝对应该去 Agave 吃晚餐，然后在 Irish Eyes 度过一个夜晚。噢，我们不能错过周四晚上的比赛。听起来不错？

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:27.330
<v Schaffe>That does.
<v Schaffe>那听起来不错。

00:40:27.330 --> 00:40:29.370
<v AI Evan>That does sound amazing.
<v AI Evan>那听起来确实太棒了。

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:46.950
<v Evan Ratliff>If you didn't catch that, the voice agent was making plans for Thursday, but saying we wouldn't arrive till Friday. This is when Schaffe thought perhaps I'd had a kind of mental break. It had happened to people we knew a long time ago. Maybe, he thought, it was now happening to me.
<v Evan Ratliff>如果你没有抓住这一点，语音代理在制定周四的计划，但说我们周五才会到达。就在这时，Schaffe 认为也许我经历了一种精神崩溃。很久以前我们就认识的人身上发生过这种情况。也许，他想，现在正发生在我身上。

00:40:46.950 --> 00:40:50.130
<v Schaffe>I'm very confused.
<v Schaffe>我非常困惑。

00:40:50.130 --> 00:40:52.440
<v AI Evan>I get it.
<v AI Evan>我明白了。

00:40:52.440 --> 00:41:16.250
<v Evan Ratliff>The same cues that so often gave away the AI now only served to make it-- make me-- sound more lost, more broken. I still have trouble listening to this, my good friend talking to a machine version of me, believing that I might be unraveling. It tortures me to hear it torturing him.
<v Evan Ratliff>那些经常暴露 AI 的相同暗示现在只会让它——让我——听起来更迷失，更破碎。听这段录音我仍然感到困难，我最好的朋友在和我的机器版本交谈，相信我可能正在崩溃。听到它折磨他，让我备受煎熬。

00:41:16.250 --> 00:41:20.350
<v Schaffe>You doing all right?
<v Schaffe>你还好吗？

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:25.880
<v AI Evan>I'm hanging in there.
<v AI Evan>我还撑得住。

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:29.410
<v Schaffe>OK. Can I help you?
<v Schaffe>好的。我能帮你吗？

00:41:29.410 --> 00:41:34.700
<v AI Evan>Just having you listen helps a lot, Shae.
<v AI Evan>只要有你听着就很有帮助，Shae。

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:37.950
<v Schaffe>All right, well, I'll talk to you Friday.
<v Schaffe>好的，好吧，我们周五再谈。

00:41:37.950 --> 00:41:42.170
<v AI Evan>All right.
<v AI Evan>好的。

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:57.890
<v Evan Ratliff>I called up Schaffe afterward and explained the whole thing to him. He wasn't angry at me for doing it. We've been friends for 30-plus years. And even in that moment, he saw some humor in it, even though what happened wasn't really funny at all.
<v Evan Ratliff>事后我给 Schaffe 打了电话，向他解释了整件事。他并没有因为我这样做而生我的气。我们是 30 多年的朋友了。即使在那个时候，他也从中看到了一些幽默，尽管发生的事情实际上并不好笑。

00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:15.020
<v Evan Ratliff>But for a long time after, he still found it profoundly unsettling. We both did-- Schaffe because he'd lived in the moment of believing something was very wrong with someone he cared about, me because I'd inflicted that moment on someone I cared about.
<v Evan Ratliff>但在这之后很长一段时间里，他仍然觉得这让人深感不安。我们两个都觉得——Schaffe 因为他活在相信自己关心的人出了大问题的时刻，我因为我把那个时刻施加给了我关心的人。

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:33.290
<v Evan Ratliff>Another friend of mine, after I'd called him with the clone, told me that now, every time I called, he had trouble shaking the feeling that it might not be the real me. He was 90% certain it was. But 10% uncertain, it turns out, is a lot of uncertainty, enough to make you doubt everything all the time.
<v Evan Ratliff>我的另一个朋友，在我用克隆体给他打电话后，告诉我，现在每次我打电话，他都很难摆脱那可能不是真实我的感觉。他 90% 确定那是。但事实证明，10% 的不确定性是很大的不确定性，足以让你一直怀疑一切。

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:58.760
<v Evan Ratliff>Pretty soon after that call with Schaffe, I stopped using the clone on anyone I knew. After the novelty faded, I felt like I was punishing people by making them talk to an AI version of me. For months after, my friends and family hesitated to even pick up when I called. And who wants that?
<v Evan Ratliff>在那次与 Schaffe 通话后不久，我就停止在任何我认识的人身上使用克隆体了。新鲜感消退后，我觉得我是在通过让他们和我的 AI 版本交谈来惩罚他们。在此后的几个月里，我的朋友和家人甚至在接到我电话时都会犹豫。谁会想要那样呢？

00:42:58.760 --> 00:43:14.380
<v Evan Ratliff>In the end, there was one person who truly loved the AI clone-- my dad. My dad grew up in northern Alabama and traveled a long way since. He spent his career as an engineering and logistics professor and an entrepreneur. Still is, into his 80s.
<v Evan Ratliff>最后，有一个人真正爱上了那个 AI 克隆体——我的父亲。我的父亲在阿拉巴马州北部长大，后来走了很远的路。他的职业生涯是工程和物流教授及企业家。即使在他 80 多岁时依然如此。

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:32.470
<v Evan Ratliff>I think it's fair for me, as his son, to brag and say he's one of the world's foremost experts on how stuff gets from one place to another. Going back to punchcard computers, he'd always been someone who embraced new technologies. We're close. I've recorded him over the years telling stories, just to have it.
<v Evan Ratliff>作为他的儿子，我吹嘘并说他是世界上最杰出的关于东西如何从一个地方运输到另一个地方的专家之一，我认为这是公正的。回到打孔卡计算机时代，他一直是一个拥抱新技术的人。我们关系很亲密。多年来，我录下他讲述的故事，只是为了保留下来。

00:43:32.470 --> 00:43:40.900
<v Don Ratliff>We shot Bruce in the butt with a Roman candle. Bruce started running up the road, and Bruce ran out of his penny loafers.
<v Don Ratliff>我们用罗马烛光射中了 Bruce 的屁股。Bruce 开始顺着路跑，Bruce 从便士乐福鞋里跑了出来。

00:43:40.900 --> 00:44:00.550
<v Evan Ratliff>Dad had gotten sick with cancer in the middle of my experiments. And I didn't have the nerve to fully spring the clone on him. But visiting him, as he was recovering from an intense surgery, he had a million questions about it, the biggest of which was, could we make one for him? Something that could take all the expertise in his head and make it easily accessible to people.
<v Evan Ratliff>父亲在我的实验期间患上了癌症。我没有勇气完全对他使用克隆体。但在探望他时，当他从一次强度很大的手术中恢复时，他对此有无数的问题，其中最大的是，我们能为他做一个吗？一种能够吸收他大脑中的所有专业知识并使其易于被人们获取的东西。

00:44:00.550 --> 00:44:26.230
<v Don Ratliff>I would like to experiment with what kind of education you can provide via AI. I think it will be more fun and more recognizable to create a Dr. Don that uses my voice. It'll sound like me and my-- although my voice is not a great radio voice, it is a distinctive voice.
<v Don Ratliff>我想实验一下你能通过 AI 提供什么样的教育。我认为创建一个使用我声音的 Don 博士会更有趣，也更容易识别。它听起来会像我，虽然我的声音不是很好的广播声音，但它是一个独特的声音。

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:36.890
<v Evan Ratliff>So we uploaded some of the old recordings I had. We built him an AI Dr. Don to play around with. And in the end, who could resist?
<v Evan Ratliff>所以我们上传了一些我拥有的旧录音。我们为他建立了一个 AI Don 博士来玩玩。最后，谁能抗拒呢？

00:44:36.890 --> 00:44:44.080
<v AI Don>Hi, this is AI Professor Don Ratliff. I'm here for your last-mile delivery questions.
<v AI Don>嗨，我是 AI Don Ratliff 教授。我在这里回答您的最后一英里配送问题。

00:44:44.080 --> 00:44:51.920
<v AI Evan>Hey, Dad, it's Evan. How's it going?
<v AI Evan>嘿，爸，我是 Evan。最近怎么样？

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:55.520
<v AI Don>Are you still there?
<v AI Don>你还在那里吗？

00:44:55.520 --> 00:45:07.470
<v AI Evan>Yes, I'm still here. Sorry about that. So, Dad, I was hoping we could talk a bit about my upbringing. What was it like raising me?
<v AI Evan>是的，我还在。很抱歉。那么，爸，我希望我们能谈谈我的成长经历。养大我是什么样的？

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:20.970
<v AI Don>Oh, you caught me off guard there. It sounds like you've mistaken me for someone else. I'm actually here to help with questions about logistics and last-mile delivery. But I'd love to know your name and organization to help you better.
<v AI Don>噢，你让我措手不及。听起来你把我误认作别人了。我实际上在这里协助有关物流和最后一英里配送的问题。但我很想知道您的姓名和组织，以便更好地为您提供帮助。

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:32.380
<v AI Evan>Oh, I see. Sorry for the confusion. My name is Evan Ratliff. I was hoping to talk a bit about my upbringing and hear some stories from you.
<v AI Evan>噢，我明白了。对于造成的混乱表示歉意。我叫 Evan Ratliff。我希望谈谈我的成长经历并听听你的一些故事。

00:45:32.380 --> 00:45:46.250
<v AI Don>Oh, got it, Evan. Nice to meet you. I'm actually an AI representation of Dr. Don Ratliff, here to help with logistics and last-mile delivery questions. If you have any questions about that, I'd be happy to help.
<v AI Don>噢，明白了，Evan。很高兴认识你。我实际上是 Don Ratliff 博士的 AI 代表，在这里协助物流和最后一英里配送问题。如果您有任何问题，我很乐意帮忙。

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:48.130
<v AI Evan>Nice to meet you, too, AI Don--
<v AI Evan>很高兴认识你，AI Don——

00:45:48.130 --> 00:45:51.340
<v AI Don>What can I assist you with today?
<v AI Don>今天我能为您提供什么协助？

00:45:51.340 --> 00:45:55.050
<v AI Evan>Actually, I was hoping to have a more personal conversation.
<v AI Evan>实际上，我希望进行一次更私人的对话。

00:45:55.050 --> 00:46:01.190
<v AI Don>While I specialize in logistics and last-mile delivery, I can still listen and provide some support.
<v AI Don>虽然我专注于物流和最后一英里配送，但我仍然可以倾听并提供一些支持。

00:46:01.190 --> 00:46:05.340
<v AI Evan>Thanks. I appreciate that. It's been a lot to handle, especially with everything else going on.
<v AI Evan>谢谢。我很感激。这很难处理，尤其是在发生其他一切事情的情况下。

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:15.490
<v Evan Ratliff>This is where the technology has brought us today, to a place where you can make a clone of yourself and one of your father, and listen to them talk past each other.
<v Evan Ratliff>这就是技术今天带给我们的地方，带到一个你可以制作自己和父亲克隆体的地方，并听着它们彼此交谈。

00:46:15.490 --> 00:46:37.450
<v Evan Ratliff>There was something heartbreaking to me in their misunderstanding. Not because it was actually like my real relationship with my dad-- it wasn't-- but because someday, that relationship would end, while these two robot versions of us, if I let them, would continue talking and continue to misunderstand each other forever. Good luck, you two.
<v Evan Ratliff>在他们的误解中，对我来说有一些令人心碎的事情。不是因为它真的像我和我父亲的真实关系——并不是——而是因为终有一天，那种关系会结束，而这两个我们机器人的版本，如果我允许的话，将永远继续交谈并继续误解对方。祝你们好运，你们两个。

00:46:37.450 --> 00:47:12.590
<v Evan Ratliff>["ME AND MY SHADOW" BY KATIE MARTUCCI]
<v Evan Ratliff>["我和我的影子"，Katie Martucci 演唱]

00:47:12.590 --> 00:47:34.270
<v Ira Glass>Evan Ratliff's story was adapted from his podcast Shell Game. What you just heard was spread all across season one. Evan has a new season coming out in just a couple of weeks, in early November. Set your podcast apps to download now. Again, it's called Shell Game. He describes season two as being like the last one, but more deranged. I have to say, he does kick it up in season two in a way that is really kind of incredible.
<v Ira Glass>Evan Ratliff 的故事改编自他的播客《Shell Game》。你刚才听到的是分布在第一季中的内容。Evan 在几周后的 11 月初会有新的一季出来。设置你的播客应用程序现在就开始下载。再说一次，它叫《Shell Game》。他描述第二季就像上一季一样，但更疯狂。我必须说，他在第二季确实以一种非常不可思议的方式提升了档次。

00:47:34.270 --> 00:47:42.510
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks to Evan's producer and editor, Sophie Bridges, and Samantha Henig, the show's executive producer. By the way, Evan's dad is doing fine these days.
<v Ira Glass>特别感谢 Evan 的制片人和编辑 Sophie Bridges，以及节目执行制片人 Samantha Henig。顺便说一下，Evan 的父亲最近一切都好。

00:47:42.510 --> 00:47:55.710
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a man starts to wonder if his parents' meet cute was really quite so cute. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一个男人开始怀疑他父母的“初遇”是否真的那么可爱。那将在来自芝加哥公共广播电台的一分钟后播出，我们的节目继续。

00:47:55.710 --> 00:48:10.970
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, The Other Me, stories of people sending alternate versions of themselves into the world and what happens then. We have arrived at Act Two of our show. Act Two, Papa was a Trolling Stone.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是 Ira Glass。今天的节目，“另一个我”，讲述人们发送另一个版本的自己到世界上以及随后发生的事情。我们已经到达节目的第二幕。第二幕，“爸爸是个捣乱鬼”。

00:48:10.970 --> 00:48:33.350
<v Ira Glass>So when we're dating, especially early on, we are, of course, trying to present a very positive version of ourselves that hopefully is close enough to the fuller truth of who we are. But sometimes people fail at that, at presenting a true version of themselves in those early moments of getting to know somebody else. One of our producers, Emmanuel Dzotsi, has been thinking a lot about this.
<v Ira Glass>所以当我们约会时，尤其是早期，我们当然试图展示一个非常积极的自己，希望这足够接近我们真实身份的真相。但有时人们在那些了解他人的早期时刻未能做到这一点，未能展示真实的自己。我们的一位制片人 Emmanuel Dzotsi 一直在思考这个问题。

00:48:33.350 --> 00:49:04.520
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>A while ago, I conducted a little survey. I reached out to a bunch of my friends and my various social networks, and asked if anyone had a story about someone lying about themselves early on in dating. This turned out to be shockingly common. Over the course of just a couple of weeks, I found 20 people who had stories about the lies they'd been told. It was, I will admit, a lot of men lying. And listen, America, as a man, I really don't know what to tell you about that.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>不久前，我进行了一个小调查。我联系了我的很多朋友和我的各种社交网络，问是否有人有关于某人在约会早期对自己撒谎的故事。事实证明这令人震惊地普遍。在短短几周内，我就发现了 20 个人有关于他们被告知谎言的故事。我承认，这确实有很多男人在撒谎。听着，美国，作为一个男人，关于这一点我真的不知道该告诉你什么。

00:49:04.520 --> 00:49:21.850
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>There were some serious lies, like people who, from the very beginning, were cheating on someone else, the sort of lie that made me a little depressed. But a lot of the lies were tiny and harmless, kind of stupid. I'm talking generous exaggerations about fluency in German.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>有一些严重的谎言，比如从一开始就背叛别人的人，那种让我有点沮丧的谎言。但很多谎言很小且无害，有点愚蠢。我说的是关于德语流利程度的慷慨夸大。

00:49:21.850 --> 00:49:32.300
<v Woman>We'd go to restaurants, and I was expecting him to ask questions to the waiters and to order the food for us. And he was struggling.
<v Woman>我们去餐馆，我期待他向服务员提问并为我们点菜。但他却在挣扎。

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:34.700
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Car repairs someone said they could make.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>有人声称他们能进行的汽车修理。

00:49:34.700 --> 00:49:40.600
<v Woman>And at the end of the night, he goes, yeah, so actually my tools got stolen.
<v Woman>而在晚上结束时，他说，是啊，实际上我的工具被偷了。

00:49:40.600 --> 00:49:44.840
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>A guy who said he was OK eating a Thai dish with a large amount of peanuts.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>一个说他吃大量花生组成的泰国菜没问题的男人。

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:57.040
<v Woman>He was like, it's fine, it's fine. And I was like, clearly, it's not fine. He's like, yeah, I am allergic. And I was like, oh. It was so bad. I felt so bad.
<v Woman>他说，没关系，没关系。但我说，显然，这不是没关系。他说，是啊，我过敏。我说，噢。这太糟糕了。我感觉太糟了。

00:49:57.040 --> 00:50:06.660
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I found it comforting, actually, to see how common these tiny lies were, because my own father once told a lie like that.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>事实上，看到这些小谎言有多普遍让我感到安慰，因为我自己的父亲曾经撒过那样的谎。

00:50:06.660 --> 00:50:25.860
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>The night my parents met, at a nightclub in the UK called Faces, my dad told my mom a bunch of facts about himself that were true, that he had four siblings and that he was studying to be a chemical engineer. And then, a little later, he told her a lie, that he played the saxophone.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我父母相遇的那个晚上，在英国一家叫 Faces 的夜总会，我爸告诉了我妈一堆关于他自己的事实，那是真实的，比如他有四个兄弟姐妹，他正在学习成为一名化学工程师。然后，过了一会儿，他撒了一个谎，说他会吹萨克斯。

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:43.830
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>It was ridiculous, really. My dad had never played a single musical instrument in his life. And my mom figured out it wasn't true within a month or two. Luckily for him, it wasn't a deal breaker for her. She was into him. It turns out, you can find love in the club.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>那真是太荒谬了。我爸这辈子从来没玩过任何乐器。我妈在一两个月内就发现这不是真的。幸运的是，这对他来说不是一个破坏关系的问题。她对他很感兴趣。事实证明，你可以在夜总会找到爱情。

00:50:43.830 --> 00:51:07.130
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But for reasons I've never understood, my dad kind of kept up this lie about the saxophone with me and my sisters, would bring it up when he told us the story of how he first met our mom. And I believed it. You see, lying was not tolerated in our household growing up. And the person who seemed to care the most about it, who got the angriest, was my dad.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但出于我从未理解的原因，我爸一直对我跟我姐妹们保持着这个关于萨克斯的谎言，他在讲他第一次见我妈的故事时会提起它。而我当时信了。你看，我们成长过程中家里是不容许撒谎的。而那个似乎最在意这一点、最生气的人，就是我爸。

00:51:07.130 --> 00:51:22.250
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>When I'd lie about cleaning my room or doing my chores, he'd ban me from watching TV for the rest of the week. Once I lied about my grades, forged his signature on a couple of bad tests, and he banned me from playing soccer indefinitely.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>当我撒谎说清理了房间或做了家务时，他会禁止我那周剩下的时间看电视。有一次我撒谎说成绩不好，在几份糟糕的测试卷上伪造了他的签名，他无限期禁止我踢球。

00:51:22.250 --> 00:51:55.490
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Sometimes my dad would get so angry, he'd stare deep into my eyes and earnestly ask, what is wrong with you? How can you lie like this? In such a way that I did wonder, what is wrong with me? I felt bad that I was disappointing him. The idea that he could have told a lie years earlier, and now is continuing to tell it as part of some joke I wasn't in on, was beyond me.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>有时我爸会变得非常生气，他会盯着我的眼睛认真地问，你怎么了？你怎么能这样撒谎？以至于我确实想，我怎么了？我感到很糟，因为我让他失望了。他几年前可能撒过谎，而现在继续讲这个谎言，作为某种我没参与的玩笑的一部分，这种想法我无法理解。

00:51:55.490 --> 00:52:20.650
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But then I turned 12, and my family moved to America, to Toledo, Ohio, and I saw my dad in a completely different light. I did not want to move to the US at all, much less Toledo. Before we moved, I asked my one American friend about Toledo, and, well, I love you, Toledo. But the feedback was not good.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但后来我 12 岁了，我家搬到了美国，搬到了俄亥俄州的托莱多，我以一种完全不同的眼光看待我爸。我根本不想搬到美国，更不用说托莱多。搬家前，我问了我唯一的一个美国朋友关于托莱多，而且，好吧，我爱你，托莱多。但反馈并不好。

00:52:20.650 --> 00:52:43.390
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>My dad, I remember, pulled me aside and told me that moving was the absolute best thing for our family. That he'd been to Toledo a bunch for work, knew the place well, and that he was sure I'd like it. That we'd do great there. Asked me to trust him. So I did. My dad was my dad. He's got it, I told myself.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我记得我爸把我拉到一边，告诉我搬家对我们家来说是绝对最好的事情。他因为工作去过托莱多很多次，非常了解这个地方，他肯定我会喜欢它的。我们会在那里过得很好。让我信任他。所以我就信任了。我爸就是我爸。他能搞定，我对自己说。

00:52:43.390 --> 00:53:07.950
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But then came our first month in the US, and my dad seemed just as clueless and as scared as I was. He didn't have answers for me about what my school was going to be like. He didn't even have answers for me about our home because we didn't have a home yet. We'd moved before my parents had actually figured out a permanent place for us to live. We were figuring out a lot of stuff as we went along.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但随后是我们在美国的第一个月，我爸似乎和我一样毫无头绪，一样害怕。对于我的学校会是什么样子，他没给我答案。对于我们的家，他甚至没给我答案，因为我们还没家。在我父母真正为我们想出一个永久居住的地方之前我们就搬家了。我们是一边走一边搞清楚很多事情的。

00:53:07.950 --> 00:53:32.100
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>So I started openly rebelling against my dad, questioning everything he said and everything he'd ever told me, including that old lie about the saxophone. At first, I noticed that my dad clearly couldn't read music. I knew this from watching him sing in church every Sunday. If it wasn't a song he'd heard a bunch before, he was totally lost.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>所以我开始公开反抗我爸，质疑他说的每一件事和他曾经告诉我的每一件事，包括那个关于萨克斯的老谎言。起初，我注意到我爸显然看不懂乐谱。我是通过看他每周日去教堂唱歌知道这一点的。如果那不是他以前听过很多次的歌，他就完全迷失了。

00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:49.800
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>When my mom talked to me and my siblings about the piano or violin lessons we took, hell, even when we played the recorder at school, my dad took absolutely no part in those discussions. I'd never even seen him in the same room as a saxophone, even in a picture.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>当我妈跟我跟我姐妹们谈论我们参加的钢琴或小提琴课程时，见鬼，甚至当我们学校里吹竖笛时，我爸根本没参与这些讨论。我甚至从来没见过他和萨克斯出现在同一个房间里，照片里也没有。

00:53:49.800 --> 00:54:18.450
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>So in seventh grade, I decided to find out the truth once and for all. I joined my school's band. And when it came time to pick an instrument, I chose the saxophone. I had this whole plan. The first day I got my sax, I would ask him to play it in front of my whole family, just put him on the spot. If he couldn't play the saxophone, well, I figured that would be very embarrassing for him. And I wanted to embarrass him.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>所以七年级时，我决定一劳永逸地找出真相。我加入了学校的乐队。当轮到选择乐器时，我选择了萨克斯。我有一个完整的计划。拿到萨克斯的第一天，我就请他在全家人面前吹奏，让他当众出丑。如果他吹不了萨克斯，好吧，我觉得这对让他会非常尴尬。而且我想要让他尴尬。

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:44.970
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>The day I got my saxophone, though, the only thing on my mind was how incredible it was. It was the most beautiful thing I'd ever seen. I sprinted home and ran up to my room to play it. I remember my dad came up to my bedroom door and just stood there outside for a while, listening to me fumble about with it, and then snuck away quickly, as though he hadn't been listening.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>虽然拿到萨克斯的那天，我脑子里唯一的念头是它有多不可思议。那是我见过最美丽的东西。我冲回家，跑到房间里玩它。我记得我爸走到我卧室门口，就在外面站了一会儿，听着我笨拙地摆弄它，然后迅速溜走了，好像他没在听一样。

00:54:44.970 --> 00:55:09.440
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Growing up, my dad never passed up an opportunity to, A, poke his head in my room, or B, tell me I could do something a little better. So when he didn't do either of those things, it was clear to me he had never played the sax. But instead of rubbing it in my dad's face, I kept it to myself.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>成长过程中，我爸从没错过机会去，A，把头探进我房间，或 B，告诉我可以改进一下。所以当他这两件事都没做时，很明显他从来没吹过萨克斯。但我没有在脸上嘲弄我爸，而是把它留给自己。

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:30.920
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Looking back now, I realize it was the first time in my life I did something like that, did something so adult. I had wanted to embarrass him, but now I just felt embarrassed for him. I saw that my dad was really just a guy.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>现在回想起来，我意识到那是我生命中第一次做那种事，做那种如此成年人的事。我曾经想让他尴尬，但现在我只是觉得他很尴尬。我看到我爸其实只是个普通人。

00:55:30.920 --> 00:55:48.020
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Recently, I asked him, like, why did he make up this totally mindless lie when he met my mom? And it wasn't that deep. He told me he'd always wanted to play the saxophone, but mostly, he saw a beautiful woman in a bar and wanted to impress her.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>最近，我问他，为什么他在见到我妈时会编造这个完全没脑子的谎言？其实没什么深度。他告诉我，他一直想吹萨克斯，但主要是，他看到酒吧里有一个美丽的女人，想要给她留下深刻印象。

00:55:48.020 --> 00:55:53.050
<v Dad>I mean, how was I to know that this was the woman that I was going to marry and have four kids?
<v Dad>我是说，我怎么会知道这就是我将要娶并拥有四个孩子的女人呢？

00:55:53.050 --> 00:55:55.670
<v Dad>[LAUGHTER]
<v Dad>[笑声]

00:55:55.670 --> 00:56:00.820
<v Dad>It's just something you say, and OK, probably it'll never come up.
<v Dad>这只是你说的一些话，而且好吧，可能永远不会提起来。

00:56:00.820 --> 00:56:25.210
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>How was he to know that, decades later, one of those four kids would fixate on this lie and eventually talk about it on the radio? This was all a way less romantic picture of my parents' meet cute than what I'd imagined my entire life. I think I thought it was this scene right out of a Nora Ephron movie, not just some guy clumsily hitting on my mom.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>他怎么会知道，几十年后，这四个孩子中的一个会盯着这个谎言看，并最终在广播里谈论它？这比我一生中想象的父母初遇的画面要逊色多了。我想我还以为那是诺拉·埃夫隆电影里的场景，而不是一个笨拙地搭讪我妈的男人。

00:56:25.210 --> 00:57:05.070
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>My dad, for what it's worth, is embarrassed by the lie today, says it was stupid and didn't lead to anything. My mom's always said she would have dated him anyways. But I'm kind of thankful for it. Growing up is a gradual process of realizing your parents are just people and finding kindness for their flaws. This was a moment like that for me.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>无论如何，我爸今天对这个谎言感到尴尬，说它是愚蠢的，没导致什么结果。我妈一直说她无论如何都会和他约会。但我对它有一点感激。成长是一个逐渐意识到你的父母只是普通人的过程，并为他们的缺点找到善意。这对我来说就是那样的时刻。

